WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.682
(bright music)

2
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:06.453
Welcome back.

3
00:00:06.453 --> 00:00:10.880
Our next guest is a Wildflower who lives a dual life.

4
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.770
She's a sanitation worker for the city of New York by day,

5
00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:18.720
and a beauty pageant contestant in her free time.

6
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.440
When you see her, you will understand why.

7
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.420
She's from Staten Island, New York;

8
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:27.684
please welcome the clean queen, Nicole Doz.

9
00:00:27.684 --> 00:00:31.213
(audience cheers)

10
00:00:31.213 --> 00:00:33.200
Hi, thank you so much for having me.

11
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.370
Thank you so much for coming here.

12
00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:41.050
What a polarity, multifaceted life you live.

13
00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.560
You keep the city clean,

14
00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.750
you're really passionate about this.

15
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.840
Tell me why, and how you go about it.

16
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.550
Yeah, so I started as a sanitation worker,

17
00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.570
which means that, yeah,

18
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.360 line:15%
I was physically picking up garbage every day,

19
00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.650 line:15%
so that would be almost approximately 12 tons a day,

20
00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:00.850 line:15%
if not more, and I also drive a street sweeper.

21
00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:02.610
What was the catalyst?

22
00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:05.530
So my dad is actually a sanitation worker also,

23
00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:07.900
and I really just wanted to be able to support myself.

24
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.250
And I knew it was a male dominated field,

25
00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.630
but I said, if they can do it, why can't I?

26
00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:13.810
I want to support myself,

27
00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:16.280
I want to be able to stand on my own two feet in this city,

28
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.550
and what better way than doing this?

29
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.090
Not only is it male dominated,

30
00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.390
even when it comes to the uniforms,

31
00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.440
you kind of had to struggle there, right?

32
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:25.273
Oh, yeah.

33
00:01:25.273 --> 00:01:27.940
So even now, there's only about 200 women,

34
00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:30.610
and there's about 7,000 sanitation workers.

35
00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.860 line:15%
And at the time when I first started working,

36
00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:35.020 line:15%
there were no female clothing,

37
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.530 line:15%
so I actually had male pants tailored to me

38
00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.920
in order just to go to work every single day.

39
00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.340
Thankfully we've come very far,

40
00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.060
so we have female pants now,

41
00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.810
I get to wear my lady pants to work,

42
00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.740
but yeah, it was a struggle

43
00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.980
that I wasn't necessarily prepared for at the beginning.

44
00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.501
My God, I'm so in awe

45
00:01:52.501 --> 00:01:54.380
(audience applauds)
of everything

46
00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:55.330
that you're saying.

47
00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:00.050
I'd also like to learn from you,

48
00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:04.190
because we get told these really,

49
00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:07.350
sometimes scary facts about recycling.

50
00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:11.050
What can we do to recycle better?

51
00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:12.390
How does it function?

52
00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.150
What do you need from us?

53
00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.370
Please teach us.

54
00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:17.990
Just to be vigilant.

55
00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:20.630
There's a lot of things that can be recycled

56
00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:23.060
that you wouldn't necessarily assume could be,

57
00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:24.090
but on the flip side of that,

58
00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:25.370
there are a lot of things that need

59
00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.680
to be recycled in very specific ways.

60
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.330
Okay, we have some examples over here.

61
00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:34.020
In my personal house, I want to learn from you.

62
00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.970
Teach me.
So we have here,

63
00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:37.410
we've got our metal.

64
00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:38.790
This is tin foil,

65
00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.630
and a lot of people would naturally assume

66
00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:41.590
that it's garbage;

67
00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.660
you've got your food on it, things like that.

68
00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:46.210
I would suggest, rinse it off, you can recycle this,

69
00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:47.520 line:15%
this would be considered metal.

70
00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.340 line:15%
Really?
Absolutely.

71
00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:50.920 line:15%
Right in your plastic and metal.

72
00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.890 line:15%
How important is cleaning a vessel for the recycling bin?

73
00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.270
I would absolutely suggest

74
00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.420
to rinse off your products as much as you can,

75
00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.730
because all of that organic matter on the inside,

76
00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:03.360
sometimes we're not able to take it

77
00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:04.980
if it's contaminated for recycling,

78
00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.250
so always do your best

79
00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:07.470 line:15%
to try to clear things out of it.

80
00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:09.430 line:15%
Like a jar of peanut butter, for example,

81
00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:10.760 line:15%
clean that all out.

82
00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.930 line:15%
But you know, whatever you can do is always helpful.

83
00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:15.080
Okay, next?

84
00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.140
Yes, so we have here, right over here,

85
00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:20.330
we have a container of milk.

86
00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:21.970
A lot of people would assume

87
00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:24.820
that because it's made of a cardboard type of material,

88
00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.410
that it's paper.

89
00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:29.830
It's not; this is also recycled with your plastic and metal,

90
00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:32.740
because on the inside, it's lined with plastic.

91
00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:35.980
So that's why you recycle this with your plastic and metal.

92
00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:37.240
Okay, okay.

93
00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:39.510
So, carton, but treated as plastic.

94
00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:40.395
Yes.
Okay.

95
00:03:40.395 --> 00:03:42.290
Recyclable plastic.
What is this?

96
00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:44.630
So that is tissue paper.

97
00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:46.190 line:15%
You hear the word paper,

98
00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:49.270 line:15%
so you would automatically assume it's recyclable.

99
00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:52.720 line:15%
This is something we like to call wish-cycling:

100
00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.290 line:15%
something we really, really hope is recyclable,

101
00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:56.760
but unfortunately, it's not.

102
00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.480 line:15%
Because of the way it's made,

103
00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.440 line:15%
this is something we're not able to recycle,

104
00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.040 line:15%
so that's something that would go in the trash.

105
00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:04.270 line:15%
What are these?

106
00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:05.964
I mean, I know what they are,

107
00:04:05.964 --> 00:04:08.360
(audience members chuckle)
but why are they here?

108
00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:11.170
These can be recycled with your paper, and they should be,

109
00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:13.800
because cardboard is so easily recyclable.

110
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.240
Okay, so these go in the recycling,

111
00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:17.940
not your little trash can

112
00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.180
next to your--
They go in the recycling.

113
00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:20.150
All right, well, thank you.

114
00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:22.530
I did not know that, and that is going to change my life.

115
00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.010
What are these guys doing?

116
00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:27.290
So, another wish-cycling item.

117
00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:29.500
They're glass, so we're automatically going to assume

118
00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:32.350
they're gonna go into your plastic, metal, glass bin,

119
00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.830
but they don't, unfortunately, that's trash.

120
00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:36.110
What's the difference?

121
00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:38.390
Different types of materials of glass.

122
00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:41.660
It's just particulars as far as recycling goes.

123
00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:44.770
All right, well, this has been honestly so educational

124
00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:45.603
for me,
(audience applauds)

125
00:04:45.603 --> 00:04:49.910
I really, really, really, appreciate it.

126
00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:54.110
Now sit back down for one more minute.

127
00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:58.150
I want to ask you, as we close out our discussion,

128
00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:01.090
give me the PM beauty queen perspective.

129
00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:03.040
What is that life like?

130
00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:06.150
That is my glamorous side, and I love every bit of it.

131
00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:08.690
I've been competing in pageants since I was about 18.

132
00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:11.020
It took me a long time until I finally won one,

133
00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:12.960
it took about six years or so,

134
00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.390
but I love it because I get

135
00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:17.210
to promote something that's important to me,

136
00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:18.650
and environmentalism

137
00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:22.200
and living a zero waste lifestyle is important to me,

138
00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:24.180
and it's really fun that I've been able

139
00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:27.860
to mesh together these two really different lifestyles

140
00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:29.350
into something really wonderful.

141
00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:32.350
(audience applauds)

142
00:05:34.693 --> 00:05:37.920
You know, that song like "I'm Every Woman"?

143
00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:39.800
I think it was written about you.

144
00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:41.450
Thank you.
Thank you so much

145
00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:43.264
for coming on this show

146
00:05:43.264 --> 00:05:44.720
(audience applauds)
and being who you are,

147
00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:46.690
and teaching what you teach,

148
00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:48.340
and being the example.

149
00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:49.510
I'm mesmerized by you.

150
00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:51.725
Thank you so much, Nicole.
Thank you.

151
00:05:51.725 --> 00:05:54.308 line:15%
(bright music)

